import{_ as t,o,c as r,a3 as i}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Depot-Related Priority Phenomena","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"仓库相关优先级现象","archivedAt":"2026-11-23","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=pgnaI2HtL8UdrJqoloJQvA","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/depot-related-priority-phenomena"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/depot-related-priority-phenomena"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/depot-related-priority-phenomena"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/depot-related-priority-phenomena"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"depot-related-priority-phenomena.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-pgnaI2HtL8UdrJqoloJQvA.md"}'),a={name:"depot-related-priority-phenomena.md"};function n(s,e,p,d,h,l){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div class="logistics-content"><h1 id="depot-related-priority-phenomena" tabindex="-1">Depot-Related Priority Phenomena <a class="header-anchor" href="#depot-related-priority-phenomena" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Depot-Related Priority Phenomena&quot;">​</a></h1><p class="page-updated" data-update-status="recorded"><span>Content updated: </span><a href="/endfield-skydoc/changelog/capture-2026-11-23T06-44-45-722Z-4358a738" title="Date when the mirror captured this page’s latest content change (China Standard Time), not the author’s edit time. View the update record." aria-label="Content updated: 2026-11-23. Date when the mirror captured this page’s latest content change (China Standard Time), not the author’s edit time. View the update record."><time datetime="2026-11-23T06:44:47.018Z">2026-11-23</time></a><span class="page-updated-zone"> (China Standard Time)</span></p><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>pgnaI2HtL8UdrJqoloJQvA</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=pgnaI2HtL8UdrJqoloJQvA" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-d5922f12e457a433.q90-04b897c52e14153c.webp" alt="Image"></td><td><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-fd14ff7c64c73e3f.q90-e2565d9429a53c5e.webp" alt="Image"></td></tr></tbody></table><p>In this structure, placing the two Depot Unloaders A and B again, together with the belt connected to A, gives A priority.</p><p>Replacing the withdrawal port on its own can put it in a low-priority state. The outcome and the number of attempts are unpredictable, so the priority issue appears essentially random.</p><p>Tests show that this phenomenon (Unloader priority) is almost always corrected after logging out, and it can usually be ignored. However, that conclusion cannot be made reliable; sometimes logging out even triggers the problem instead.</p><p>It is uncertain whether Reactor Crucibles exhibit a similar phenomenon (Unloader priority).</p><p>Depot Loaders exhibit the same input-priority phenomenon as Reactor Crucibles: first connected, first input.</p><p>In addition, the Protocol Automation-Core (PAC) exhibits the same phenomena (Unloader priority and Loader priority), and this behavior may be stable.</p><p>November 2026, by 13x286. A hypothesis about how priority arises, based on my work on deposit ports. The withdrawal-port explanation is speculative.</p><ol><li><p>The factory may be processed sequentially rather than all at once. Storage often receives mixed belts, so its deposits and withdrawals may be single-threaded, though withdrawals could still be grouped by item type.</p></li><li><p>To save computation, the game may skip unused ports on the core and storage ports without a belt or logistics component attached.</p></li><li><p>Each entire conveyor belt is processed as one facility, judging from its contribution to the facility limit and similar evidence.</p></li></ol><p>The hypothesis for <strong>deposit ports</strong> is that when a deposit port on storage or a core is connected to a belt or logistics component, it is assigned an ID. Deposits at the same time are processed in ID order.</p><p>Replacing a deposit port or editing the belt or component immediately attached to it would assign it a new ID and could change its priority. Changing the connection at the far end would have no effect.</p><p><strong>Withdrawal ports</strong> behave differently. Extending the deposit hypothesis, ordinary withdrawal ports might use a numbered round-robin order. With empty storage and three ordinary outputs 1, 2, and 3, one incoming belt gives order 1, 2, 3. Depositing two items produces outputs at 1 and 2. Simultaneously depositing via three belts produces outputs at 1, 2, and 3. Depositing one more item then produces output at 3. This suggests a round-robin position that survives between cycles and across long gaps, consistent with a persistent counter or ID.</p><p>How are the initial withdrawal IDs assigned? One possibility is to inspect the IDs already in use. With an equal-priority cycle ABCD, a new port E appears last, giving ABCDE. We can distinguish first from last because E outputs in that same cycle. Adding E and F in succession would give ABCDFEABCD if each went first, but the observed order is ABCDEFABCD. Deleting a belt when the exiting item is half a tile from its port also causes the next port to be skipped. <strong>My guess is that after removing C from ABCDE, the pointer still advances to position four, now E, and skips D.</strong></p><p>I used the earlier procedure for making withdrawal port A reliably take priority on two separate A/B pairs. The two A ports took turns independently of the others, and after removing them the two B ports took turns. This suggests withdrawal priority may be an unintended bug <s>or maybe it&#39;s a feature</s>. <strong>When an ordinary output C already exists at the base, you cannot create A/B priority. If A already has priority, B and C should be peers, yet a second A can still be given priority.</strong> <s>Including other bases, including the Valley.</s> <strong>If another base has an ordinary output C, the A/B procedure can give B priority, with B, C, and subsequently placed outputs all ending up at the same priority.</strong> The bug may arise when existing withdrawal-port IDs are inherited, with the established C ID changing the outcome.</p><p>I then tested which steps in moving A&#39;s port, A&#39;s belt, and B&#39;s port with M are required. <strong>B&#39;s port is usually less important.</strong> With <strong>no ordinary withdrawal port</strong> in the base, moving <strong>A&#39;s port and A&#39;s belt together</strong> can give A priority, leaving new ordinary ports at low priority. To give B priority when another base already has ordinary port C, <strong>move A&#39;s port, A&#39;s belt, and B&#39;s port together</strong>. A may come out ahead, or A, B, and C may all be <strong>high priority</strong>. Testing another A/B group shows this matches A&#39;s priority. This particular case requires B&#39;s port rather than its belt. It may be related to the unusual output on moving a port in the supplementary experiments.</p><p>There are <strong>only two priority levels, high and low</strong>. Equal-priority ports have a round-robin order of their own.</p><p>Picture the round-robin order as slots with a pointer to the current slot. After port 3 outputs and before port 4 can output, remove port 3. Port 4 is skipped. Removing port 1 or 2 also works if timed correctly. Turning a withdrawal port off leaves its priority and order intact. This points to a reset tied to the number of output ports.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-1d4479d6f280c0cb.png" alt="Depot-Related Priority Phenomena"></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-81427ebe166c38b0.png" alt="Depot-Related Priority Phenomena"></p><p>Priority ports only go first. Ordinary ports still output when items are in storage. We know that priority ports take turns among themselves and ordinary ports take turns once the priority ports are removed. Do ordinary ports still take turns with the priority ports in place?</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-45c28e1f5b971fcb.png" alt="Depot-Related Priority Phenomena"></p><p>Ordinary ports still cycle evenly even if we insert a deposit of 2 items in between to reset the sequence. That complicates the pointer theory. For now, suppose the pointer works with a counter that increments when the pointer visits it.</p><p>This reminded me that the crafting manual logs plant consumption, just as the integrated aid event counted raw-material consumption. A counter and a pointer together seem a plausible way to track the sequence and current position across cycles.</p><p><s>The A/B procedure may give B such a low priority counter that it falls below the total number of output ports, so the pointer never reaches B or later ports such as B-1. After establishing A/B priority, I removed B and placed C. C did not inherit A&#39;s high priority and the A/C priority difference remained. This hypothesis failed.</s></p><p><s>The A/B procedure may give A a very high priority counter, so a pointer counting down from the top never reaches B or later ports such as B-1. I haven&#39;t figured out a test yet.</s> I&#39;m abandoning the pointer-only hypothesis. B can be reached and simply isn&#39;t visited first. This may be tied to optimization: if storage has 3 items, the pointer might look ahead for 3 eligible outputs without resetting. Or there may be no pointer reset at all. Each port could just move to the end of its priority tier after outputting, which would also give uniform round-robin service.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-521e4976b825c6e9.png" alt="Depot-Related Priority Phenomena"></p><p><strong>Further experiments:</strong></p><p>With A already prioritized, placing or copying a withdrawal port gives it lower priority than A and it cannot output. Moving one with M makes it output once at the end of the current cycle, then stop. Withdrawal priority may be linked to moving with M. Pressing M and cancelling does not count.</p><p>Under an unidentified setup using M, one belt feeds empty storage and A and B both withdraw. After every two items enter, A and B output simultaneously. The items were confirmed absent from the storage buffer, and A and B each run at 1/2 of the input rate. The state persists across clients, devices, and offline sessions, and tolerates some variation in incoming rates. <strong>An uncontrolled variable may be involved, so this is on hold.</strong></p><p>The ID idea comes from early Arknights targeting. Each operator had an aggro score based on a large constant × aggro level + time, such as 10000 × aggro level + creation time. Enemies would prefer higher aggro and then later deployment. Early creation time had a bounded range and incremented by 1 every three frames, so deployments very far apart or very close together could tie. Enemies would then pick the operator earlier in the order, with the lower ID. Endfield may reuse something similar or use revised logic. Either way, it gives us a way to start investigating the mechanics and their bugs.</p></div>',1)])])}const u=t(a,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
